### Alternative Concept – Single Class with Switch

Instead of using inheritance, all vehicle types can be represented using a single Vehicle class containing a type variable.

```cpp
enum Type {
    BICYCLE,
    TWO_WHEELER,
    CAR,
    ELECTRIC_CAR
};
```

The `charge()` function can use a switch statement:

```cpp
double charge(int days) {
    switch (type) {

        case BICYCLE:
            return dailyRate * days;

        case TWO_WHEELER:
            return dailyRate * days + 100;

        case CAR:
            return dailyRate * days + 200;

        case ELECTRIC_CAR:
            return dailyRate * days +
                   battery * chargeRate;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### Complexity Analysis

| Feature | Inheritance with Virtual Functions | Single Class with Switch |
|---|---|---|
| Time per charge calculation | O(1) | O(1) |
| Space per object | Required data and virtual pointer overhead | May contain unused data members |
| Adding a new vehicle | Create a new derived class | Modify Vehicle and switch |
| Maintenance | Easier | More complicated |
| Extensibility | High | Low |

**Conclusion:**

The inheritance approach is better because it supports runtime polymorphism and makes the program easier to maintain and extend. New vehicle types can be added without modifying the existing classes. This follows the Open/Closed Principle, which states that software should be open for extension but closed for modification.
